Certainly! Below is a step-by-step guide on how to export a collection of users to an Excel file using Laravel Excel without using the words "simple" and "easy".

### Step 1: Install Laravel Excel

First, ensure that you have installed the Laravel Excel package. Run the following command in your terminal:

```bash
composer require maatwebsite/excel
```

### Step 2: Create an Export Class

Next, create an export class that will handle the data export. You can use the Artisan command to generate this class:

```bash
php artisan make:export UsersExport --model=User
```

This command will create a new file named `UsersExport.php` in the `app/Exports` directory.

### Step 3: Define the Export Logic

Open the newly created `UsersExport.php` file and define the logic to export users. Here is an example implementation:

```php
namespace App\Exports;

use App\Models\User;
use Maatwebsite\Excel\Concerns\FromCollection;

class UsersExport implements FromCollection
{
    public function collection()
    {
        return User::all();
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Create a Route for Exporting

In your `web.php` file, define a route that will trigger the export:

```php
use App\Exports\UsersExport;
use Maatwebsite\Excel\Facades\Excel;

Route::get('export-users', function () {
    return Excel::download(new UsersExport, 'users.xlsx');
});
```

### Step 5: Test the Export

To test the export functionality, navigate to the URL defined in your route (e.g., `http://your-app-url/export-users`). This should download an Excel file named `users.xlsx` containing the list of users.

### Step 6: Customize the Export (Optional)

If you need to customize the exported file, such as adding headings or formatting, you can implement additional interfaces provided by Laravel Excel. For instance, to add headings, modify your `UsersExport.php` as follows:

```php
namespace App\Exports;

use App\Models\User;
use Maatwebsite\Excel\Concerns\FromCollection;
use Maatwebsite\Excel\Concerns\WithHeadings;

class UsersExport implements FromCollection, WithHeadings
{
    public function collection()
    {
        return User::all();
    }

    public function headings(): array
    {
        return [
            'ID',
            'Name',
            'Email',
            'Created At',
            'Updated At',
        ];
    }
}
```

### Conclusion

Following these steps will allow you to export a collection of users to an Excel file using Laravel Excel. You can further customize the export process to meet your specific needs by exploring the extensive features offered by the package.

